[5]
t=Spindle Acceleration
d=The amount by which velocity is increased at each iteration of the Interpolator if acceleration is required and the 'S' axis is involved.

[6]
t=Spindle Deceleration
d=The amount by which velocity is decreased at each iteration of the Interpolator if deceleration is required and the 'S' axis is involved.

[7]
t=Maximum Traverse Rate
d=The maximum allowable traverse rate (positioning or cutting) for the 1st (X), 2nd (Y), and 4th (C) axes.

[8]
t=Z-Axis Traverse Rate
d=The maximum allowable traverse rate (positioning or cutting) for the 3rd (Z) axis.

[9]
t=Homing Speed
d=The rate at which an axis is moved while looking for an index mark.  Must be low enough to guarantee at least a 50 uS wide index pulse.

[10]
t=Accel/Decel Profile Selection
d=0 = Normal (trapezoidal) profile using params 2 through 9.  1 = 'S'-Curve based SOLELY on parameter #1.

[11]
t=Residual Feedrate for DLT ramp
d=The feedrate used for final positioning after end-of-move ramp-down.  The rate at which we can "stop on a dime".

[12]
t=Minimum reduction of following error per Tick
d=A special deceleration parameter that is applied when any axis following error grows too large.

[13]
t=Portion of Jog following error used as FDRT per Tick
d=Jog-Knob (Handwheel) uses this to calculate a speed proportional to the knob speed to minimize run-on at the end of motion.

[14]
t=Measuring Feed
d=The feedrate at which the Touch-Probe measurements are made.

[15]
t=Probing post-hit continuation distance
d=The distance that the Touch-Probe continues to move after a touch is registered.  This is necessary for some mechanical-switch probes.

[16]
t=Touch-Probe back-off distance
d=The distance that the Touch-Probe retracts after a touch is registered.

[17]
t=X (1st) Axis move negative limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the negative (-) limit of travel for this axis.

[18]
t=X (1st) Axis move positive limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the positive (+) limit of travel for this axis.

[19]
t=Y (2nd) Axis move negative limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the negative (-) limit of travel for this axis.

[20]
t=Y (2nd) Axis move positive limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the positive (+) limit of travel for this axis.

[21]
t=Z (3rd) Axis move negative limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the negative (-) limit of travel for this axis.

[22]
t=Z (3rd) Axis move positive limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the positive (+) limit of travel for this axis.

[23]
t=C (4th) Axis move negative limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the negative (-) limit of travel for this axis.

[24]
t=C (4th) Axis move positive limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the positive (+) limit of travel for this axis.

[25]
t=S (5th) Axis move negative limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the negative (-) limit of travel for this axis.

[26] 
t=S (5th) Axis move positive limit of travel
d=The value, in machine coordinates, of the positive (+) limit of travel for this axis.

[27]
t=X (1st) Axis Home switch offset
d=The distance past the Axis-Home-Switch to travel before stopping on the next Index-Mark.  NOTE: Homing ALWAYS stops ON the index mark!

[28]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Home switch offset
d=The distance past the Axis-Home-Switch to travel before stopping on the next Index-Mark.  NOTE: Homing ALWAYS stops ON the index mark!

[29]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Home switch offset
d=The distance past the Axis-Home-Switch to travel before stopping on the next Index-Mark.  NOTE: Homing ALWAYS stops ON the index mark!

[30]
t=C (4th) Axis Home switch offset
d=The distance past the Axis-Home-Switch to travel before stopping on the next Index-Mark.  NOTE: Homing ALWAYS stops ON the index mark!

[31]
t=S (5th) Axis Home switch offset
d=The distance past the Axis-Home-Switch to travel before stopping on the next Index-Mark.  NOTE: Homing ALWAYS stops ON the index mark!

[32]
t=X (1st) Axis encoder resolution
d=The number of 10 nanometer (centa-micrometer, or CUM) units represented by each axis encoder count.  Example: 0.001 mm per count = 100 CUMs.

[33]
t=Y (2nd) Axis encoder resolution
d=The number of 10 nanometer (centa-micrometer, or CUM) units represented by each axis encoder count.  Example: 0.001 mm per count = 100 CUMs.

[34]
t=Z (3rd) Axis encoder resolution
d=The number of 10 nanometer (centa-micrometer, or CUM) units represented by each axis encoder count.  Example: 0.001 mm per count = 100 CUMs.

[35]
t=C (4th) Axis encoder resolution
d=The number of micro-degree units represented by each Axis Encoder count.

[36]
t=S (5th) Axis encoder resolution
d=The number of micro-degree units represented by each Axis Encoder count.

[37]
t=JogKnob encoder resolution
d=The number of 10 nanometer (centa-micrometer, or CUM) units represented by each handwheel encoder count.  Example: 0.001 mm per count = 100 CUMs.

[38]
t=The 'Homing Sequence' configuration parameter
b0=1st Axis Home
b1=1st Axis Home Switch
b2=Axis Home Direction
b4=2nd Axis Home
b5=2nd Axis Home Switch
b6=Axis Home Direction
b8=3rd Axis Home
b9=3rd Axis Home Switch
b10=Axis Home Direction
b12=4th Axis Home
b13=4th Axis Home Switch
b14=Axis Home Direction

[39]
t=Dead-Band (Null Zone) for Joy-Sticks
d=The unsigned value that is treated as 'centered' (no movement) for the Joy-Stick Analog Inputs.

[40]
t=Scale Factor for Joy-Sticks
d=Used to scale the analog Joy-Stick inputs for the desired response.  One scale factor is used for ALL Joy-Stick inputs.

[41]
t=X (1st) Axis Applied Motor Voltage
d=The maximum voltage that can be applied to the motor.  The Equation is Motor_Resistance * Maximum_Current.

[42]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Applied Motor Voltage
d=The maximum voltage that can be applied to the motor.  The Equation is Motor_Resistance * Maximum_Current.

[43]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Applied Motor Voltage
d=The maximum voltage that can be applied to the motor.  The Equation is Motor_Resistance * Maximum_Current.

[44]
t=C (4th) Axis Applied Motor Voltage
d=The maximum voltage that can be applied to the motor.  The Equation is Motor_Resistance * Maximum_Current.

[45]
t=Spindle Velocity Filter Period 
d=The time period over which spindle velocity is averaged.

[46]
t=Advanced Servo Options
d=This parameter is 8 4-bit fields that enable/disable specific servo-system features.
b0=This parameter is 
b1=8 4-bit fields that 
b2=enable/disable specific
b3=servo-system features.

[47]
t=C-Axis Traverse Rate
d=The maximum allowable traverse rate (positioning or cutting) for the 4th (C) axis.

[48]
t=Spindle Accel/Decel Rate
d=The Open-Loop Spindle Accel/Decel rate specified in RPM per Second.  This is the rate at which speed changes occur.

[49]
t=Transmission Shift Speed
d=The speed, in RPM at the motor shaft, at which gear changes occur.

[50]
t=In-Position Tolerance
d=The maximum distance from exact position allowable before a move is considered done and the next move can start.

[51]
t=Following Error Shutdown Point
d=The amount of axis following error allowed before a Following-Error Alarm is generated.

[52]
t=Maximum Following Error
d=The amount of axis following error allowed before feedrate pro-rating occurs.

[53]
t=Maximum Spindle Slip
d=Maximum allowable slip, expressed as a percentage of spindle speed, above which a Spindle-Stall Alarm will be generated.

[54]
t=Spindle Slip Limit
d=Maximum allowable slip, expressed as a percentage of spindle speed, above which the feedrate will be reduced in an effort to reduce spindle loading.

[55]
t=X (1st) Axis Backlash
d=The amount of extra motion to generate at axis reversal to take up mechanical backlash between the motor (and encoder) and the axis.

[56]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Backlash
d=The amount of extra motion to generate at axis reversal to take up mechanical backlash between the motor (and encoder) and the axis.

[57]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Backlash
d=The amount of extra motion to generate at axis reversal to take up mechanical backlash between the motor (and encoder) and the axis.

[58]
t=C (4th) Axis Backlash
d=The amount of extra motion to generate at axis reversal to take up mechanical backlash between the motor (and encoder) and the axis.

[59]
t=S (5th) Axis Backlash
d=The amount of extra motion to generate at axis reversal to take up mechanical backlash between the motor (and encoder) and the axis.

[60]
t=X (1st) Axis Velocity Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) for the velocity command output by the Servo.  If the axis is not installed, this parameter should be zero.

[61]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Velocity Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) for the velocity command output by the Servo.  If the axis is not installed, this parameter should be zero.

[62]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Velocity Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) for the velocity command output by the Servo.  If the axis is not installed, this parameter should be zero.

[63]
t=C (4th) Axis Velocity Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) for the velocity command output by the Servo.  If the axis is not installed, this parameter should be zero.

[64]
t=S (5th) Axis Velocity Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) for the velocity command output by the Servo.  If the axis is not installed, this parameter should be zero.

[65]
t=X (1st) Axis Servo Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the command to create the value loaded into the axis DAC.  It is the number of right-shifts.

[66]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Servo Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the command to create the value loaded into the axis DAC.  It is the number of right-shifts.

[67]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Servo Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the command to create the value loaded into the axis DAC.  It is the number of right-shifts.

[68]
t=C (4th) Axis Servo Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the command to create the value loaded into the axis DAC.  It is the number of right-shifts.

[69]
t=S (5th) Axis Servo Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the command to create the value loaded into the axis DAC.  It is the number of right-shifts.

[70]
t=Spindle Velocity Sample Period
d=The number of 250 usec periods to sum Spindle velocities.  Counts must be summed over time to attain the desired 1 RPM resolution.

[71]
t=Spindle Command Gain
d=Scale factor for Spindle speed commands - formula for Spindle Gain is:  SpindleGain = (MaxDacCnt/2) x (MaxDesiredVoltage/10) x (MaxRPM/4096).

[72]
t=Spindle-Motor Minimum RPM
d=The minimum speed of the spindle motor.  Used for calculating gear range.

[73]
t=Spindle-Motor Maximum RPM
d=The maximum speed of the spindle motor.  Used for calculating gear range.

[74]
t=Spindle-Range #1 Maximum RPM
d=The maximum speed of the spindle in gear range #1.  Used for calculating necessary gear range.

[75]
t=Spindle-Range #2 Maximum RPM
d=The maximum speed of the spindle in gear range #2.  Used for calculating necessary gear range.

[76]
t=Spindle-Range #3 Maximum RPM
d=The maximum speed of the spindle in gear range #3.  Used for calculating necessary gear range.

[77]
t=Integral Following Error Gain
d=Used during a reversal in the direction of travel. It allows the accumulated integral following error to "slew" rapidly to a new stable state. Too large: causes bumping during reversal. Too low: swings the axis wide during reversal.

[78]
t=Change-In-Direction Counter Initializer
d=Initializes a counter when the axes reverse direction. If non-zero, the Integral Following Error Gain is used instead of the normal gain for the integral following error term.  Too large: stability problems. Too low: bumps on reversal.

[79]
t=Normal Gain For Integral Following Error Term
d=Main term for the position loop. If this gain is too large, the axis will oscillate slowly. If this gain is too low, the system may have excessive following errors and/or may not adjust to changing following errors quickly enough.

[80]
t=Following Error Integral Term Magnitude Limiter
d=Limits the following error integral term "magnitude". If too low, the integral term may not grow large enough to push the axis into position. If the integral term grows too large, it masks severe mechanical problems until something breaks.

[81]
t=Integral Following Error Loss
d=Used to "drain" the integral following error term. This term is not usually used.

[82]
t=Spindle Analog-Feedback Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the spindle-velocity feedback to calculate spindle RPM.

[83]
t=Lube Pump Interval
d=The time, in seconds of axis motion, between activations of the lubrication pump.  A value of zero specifies always on.

[84]
t=Lube Pump Duration
d=The time, in seconds, that the lubricator pump will run before the pressure test is initiated.

[85]
t=X Axis Clearance Point
d=The position, in absolute machine coordinates, to which the X axis will move for when commanded to move to Clearance-Point (eg M22, M26, etc.).

[86]
t=Y Axis Clearance Point
d=The position, in absolute machine coordinates, to which the Y axis will move for when commanded to move to Clearance-Point (eg M22, M26, etc.).

[87]
t=X Center-Of-Rotation for Rotary Axis
d=Used by the control to calculate constant surface speed for rotary axis.  Value is in absolute machine coordinates.

[88]
t=Y Center-Of-Rotation for Rotary Axis
d=Used by the control to calculate constant surface speed for rotary axis.  Value is in absolute machine coordinates.

[89]
t=Z Center-Of-Rotation for Rotary Axis
d=Used by the control to calculate constant surface speed for rotary axis.  Value is in absolute machine coordinates.

[90]
t=Tool-Changer Number Of Tools
d=Number of tool-pockets in tool-changer magazine.  A value of zero denotes no automatic tool-changer installed.

[91]
t=Tool-Changer Configuration
d=0=Lo polarity, Air-Arm 'Lightning'   1=Lo polarity, Air-Arm 'Lightning'  8=Hi polarity, Motor-Arm 'Discovery' 9=Hi polarity, Motor-Arm 'Discovery'

[92]
t=Tool-Changer Orient Position
d=Rotational offset from index-mark of spindle tool-change position.

[93]
t=Tool-Changer Z-Axis Offset
d=Linear offset (in absolute machine coordinates) from Home position of Z-Axis tool-change position.

[94]
t=Jogging Feedrate
d=The feedrate in effect for Jog Continuous.  This feedrate overrides the normal feedrate maximum(s) installed via parameters #7 and #8.

[95]
t=Swarf Conveyor Reversal Time
d=The time, in seconds, that the Swarf Conveyor will run in reverse (M37) mode.

[96]
t=Tool-Changer Carousel Delay Time
d=The number of 8.89 ms timer periods that the TLAUF board should delay before turning the Carousel Rotation Motor off.

[97]
t=Time-Out for PLC Driver
d=The time allowed for commands to an external Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) to execute a command ('S', 'T', or 'M').

[98]
t=The 'Master' configuration parameter
b0=Family type
b1=Spindle type
b2=Spnd Feedback
b3=Species
b4=Spnd Power Feedback
b5=Feedrate-Override-Source
b6=Spindle-Override-Source
b7=Aux Function Handling
d=This parameter consists of 8 4-bit fields that define basic machine capabilities.

[99]
t=X (1st) Axis Home Position
d=The value, in absolute machine coordinates, loaded into the 1st (X) axis position registers at completion of Find-Home command.

[100]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Home Position
d=The value, in absolute machine coordinates, loaded into the 2nd (Y) axis position registers at completion of Find-Home command.

[101]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Home Position
d=The value, in absolute machine coordinates, loaded into the 3rd (Z) axis position registers at completion of Find-Home command.

[102]
t=C (4th) Axis Home Position
d=The value, in absolute machine coordinates, loaded into the 4th (C) axis position registers at completion of Find-Home command.

[103]
t=S (5th) Axis Home Position
d=The value, in absolute machine coordinates, loaded into the 5th (S) axis position registers at completion of Find-Home command.

[104]
t=Tool-Change Clearance
d=The height above tool-change (tool-holder height).  The value necessary to move the Z axis DURING tool change (eg to effect tool-ejection).

[105]
t=1st (X) Axis Leadscrew Compensation
d=A 'fractional-count' value used to implement leadscrew-length compensation.  The equation is:  leadComp = ((idealRes*ideadDist)/realDist)-idealRes.

[106]
t=2nd (Y) Axis Leadscrew Compensation
d=A 'fractional-count' value used to implement leadscrew-length compensation.  The equation is:  leadComp = ((idealRes*ideadDist)/realDist)-idealRes.

[107]
t=3rd (Z) Axis Leadscrew Compensation
d=A 'fractional-count' value used to implement leadscrew-length compensation.  The equation is:  leadComp = ((idealRes*ideadDist)/realDist)-idealRes.

[108]
t=4th (C) Axis Leadscrew Compensation
d=A 'fractional-count' value used to implement leadscrew-length compensation.  The equation is:  leadComp = ((idealRes*ideadDist)/realDist)-idealRes.

[109]
t=Spindle Horsepower Limit - Power
d=Desired power limit, in horsepower.

[110]
t=Spindle Horsepower Limit - Speed
d=Spindle speed, in RPM, above which the Spindle-Power Limiter operates.

[111]
t=Spindle Current Sense Gain
d=The scale factor (gain) used to divide the spindle-current feedback to calculate spindle load.

[112]
t=Axis Run-Away Test Velocity Difference
d=The maximum allowable difference, expressed as a percentage, between DESIRED and ACTUAL velocity.

[113]
t=Axis Run-Away Test Hysteresis
d=The maximum allowable time, expressed in 10 ms periods, that the velocity difference may be exceeded before a Run-Away Alarm is generated.

[114]
t=X (1st) Axis Differential Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Differential Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[115]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Differential Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Differential Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[116]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Differential Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Differential Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[117]
t=C (4th) Axis Differential Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Differential Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[118]
t=S (5th) Axis Differential Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Differential Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[119]
t=X (1st) Axis Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[120]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[121]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[122]
t=C (4th) Axis Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[123]
t=S (5th) Axis Following Error Gain
d=This parameter is used to scale the Following Error for the PWM Drive system.

[124]
t=Maximum X (1st) Axis PWM Deviation
d=The maximum deviation the PWM signal to the Amplifiers may take from the nominal PWM value required to maintain the present axis velocities.

[125]
t=Maximum Y (2nd) Axis PWM Deviation
d=The maximum deviation the PWM signal to the Amplifiers may take from the nominal PWM value required to maintain the present axis velocities.

[126]
t=Maximum Z (3rd) Axis PWM Deviation
d=The maximum deviation the PWM signal to the Amplifiers may take from the nominal PWM value required to maintain the present axis velocities.

[127]
t=Maximum C (4th) Axis PWM Deviation
d=The maximum deviation the PWM signal to the Amplifiers may take from the nominal PWM value required to maintain the present axis velocities.

[128]
t=Z Axis Velocity Balance Flag
d=This value modifies the Z axis velocity feed-forward gain for downward z axis motion.  For most machines this value should be set to zero.  For sliding head machines, such as Torque-Cut, set to 15.

[129]
t=X (1st) Axis Motor Volts Per 1000 RPM
d=The DC axis motors Back EMF (Kev) rating in volts per 1000 RPM.

[130]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Motor Volts Per 1000 RPM
d=The DC axis motors Back EMF (Kev) rating in volts per 1000 RPM.

[131]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Motor Volts Per 1000 RPM
d=The DC axis motors Back EMF (Kev) rating in volts per 1000 RPM.

[132]
t=C (4th) Axis Motor Volts Per 1000 RPM
d=The DC axis motors Back EMF (Kev) rating in volts per 1000 RPM.

[133]
t=S (5th) Axis Motor Volts Per 1000 RPM
d=The DC axis motors Back EMF (Kev) rating in volts per 1000 RPM.

[134]
t=X (1st) Axis Encoder Lines Per Motor Revolution
d=The number of encoder lines per motor revolution.  This value is DIFFERENT than the encoder resolution !!!!

[135]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Encoder Lines Per Motor Revolution
d=The number of encoder lines per motor revolution.  This value is DIFFERENT than the encoder resolution !!!!

[136]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Encoder Lines Per Motor Revolution
d=The number of encoder lines per motor revolution.  This value is DIFFERENT than the encoder resolution !!!!

[137]
t=C (4th) Axis Encoder Lines Per Motor Revolution
d=The number of encoder lines per motor revolution.  This value is DIFFERENT than the encoder resolution !!!!

[138]
t=S (5th) Axis Encoder Lines Per Motor Revolution
d=The number of encoder lines per motor revolution.  This value is DIFFERENT than the encoder resolution !!!!

[139]
t=Bus Voltage For Pwm Amplifier
d=The bus voltage for the PWM amplifiers is typically 120 volts DC.

[140]
t=Pwm Offset For X,Y,Z,W Axis
d=The DC brush motors require a minimum voltage for any motion to be generated.   PwmOffset = 0.75 * Voffset * Maximum Pwm Cnt Vbus

[141]
t=Pwm Amp Load Integrator Limit
d=This parameter sets the time constant for the digital-drive load-limiter. An alarm will be posted if the load-limit is exceeded.

[142]
t=PWM Digital Filter Constant #1
d=The digital filter constant (shift amount) used to smooth the PWM outputs.

[143]
t=PWM Digital Filter Constant #2
d=The digital filter constant (shift amount) used to smooth the PWM outputs.

[144]
t=PWM Digital Filter Constant #3
d=The digital filter constant (shift amount) used to smooth the PWM outputs.

[145]
t=PWM Digital Filter Constant #4
d=The digital filter constant (shift amount) used to smooth the PWM outputs.

[146]
t=X (1st) Axis Lead-Comp Interval
d=The distance between compensation points.  AXIS TRAVEL DIVIDED BY LEAD COMP INTERVAL MUST BE LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO 256 !!!

[147]
t=Y (2nd) Axis Lead-Comp Interval
d=The distance between compensation points.  AXIS TRAVEL DIVIDED BY LEAD COMP INTERVAL MUST BE LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO 256 !!!

[148]
t=Z (3rd) Axis Lead-Comp Interval
d=The distance between compensation points.  AXIS TRAVEL DIVIDED BY LEAD COMP INTERVAL MUST BE LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO 256 !!!

[149]
t=C (4th) Axis Lead-Comp Interval
d=The distance between compensation points.  AXIS TRAVEL DIVIDED BY LEAD COMP INTERVAL MUST BE LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO 256 !!!
